  "worked_solution": "According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, the magnitude of the inward gravitational force pulling the object toward the planet is\n\n$$\n{F}_{\\text{in}}=G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $G$ is the gravitational constant, $m$ is the object's mass, and $M$ is the planet's mass.\n\nSince this is the only force and it acts inward, the resultant force, taking *outward* as positive, is $F=-{F}_{\\text{in}}.$\nApplying Newton's second law, $F=ma,$ where $a$ is the acceleration of the object, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =-{F}_{\\text{in}} \\\\ ma & =-G\\,\\frac{mM}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ a & =-\\frac{GM}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the acceleration is inversely proportional to the square of the distance,\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{k}{{r}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwith constant of proportionality $k=GM>0.$\n\nWhen at the surface, $r=(6.1\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{m},$ the acceleration of the object due to gravity is $a=-8.3\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ Substituting into our previous equation, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8.3 & =-\\frac{k}{(6.1\\times {10}^{7})^{2}} \\\\ k & =8.3\\cdot (6.1\\times {10}^{7})^{2} \\\\  & =8.3\\cdot (6.1)^{2}\\times ({10}^{7})^{2} \\\\  & =308.843\\times {10}^{14} \\\\  & =3.08843\\times {10}^{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting back into the equation, we get\n\n$$\na=-\\frac{3.08843\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since acceleration $a$ is the derivative of velocity $v,$ which is the derivative of distance $r,$ we can express acceleration as follows using the chain rule, treating $v$ as a function of $r$ along the trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\  & =\\frac{dr}{dt}\\cdot \\frac{dv}{dr} \\\\  & =v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, substituting this result into our equation, we obtain a separable differential equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\nv\\frac{dv}{dr}=-\\frac{3.08843\\times {10}^{16}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$",
